        <article id="jetson-4-1-tl-prep" class="module-card">
          <div class="module-header">
            <span class="module-badge">Module 4-1 • Swap Memory, USB Mount &amp; OpenImages Dataset</span>
            <span style="font-family:var(--font-mono); font-size:11.5px; color:var(--text-muted);">4GB Swap • exFAT USB • mobilenet-v1-ssd</span>
          </div>
          <h3 class="module-title">4-1. Mount Swap Memory, Attach USB Storage &amp; Download OpenImages (4-1. Transfer Learning 준비)</h3>
          <p>
            PyTorch SSD training exceeds the Jetson Nano's 4 GB shared RAM unless disk swap is enabled. Allocate a 4 GB swap file on the host, install exFAT drivers, mount an external USB drive into the Docker container with <code>--volume</code>, and download the pretrained MobileNet-V1 SSD checkpoint and OpenImages dataset subset:
          </p>

          <div class="cmd-block">
            <div class="cmd-top">
              <span>1. Expand Swap Memory &amp; Mount exFAT USB Drive into Docker</span>
              <button class="copy-btn" onclick="copyCmd(this)">Copy</button>
            </div>
            <pre># Expand swap file (on host Ubuntu terminal)
git clone https://github.com/JetsonHacksNano/installSwapfile
cd installSwapfile &amp;&amp; ./installSwapfile.sh
free -m

# Enable exFAT USB support and launch Docker with USB volume mounted
sudo apt-get install exfat-fuse exfat-utils
cd ~/jetson-inference
docker/run.sh --volume /media/mobis/mobis_usb:/media/mobis/mobis_usb</pre>
          </div>

          <div class="cmd-block">
            <div class="cmd-top">
              <span>2. Download Base SSD Checkpoint &amp; OpenImages Subset (Inside Docker)</span>
              <button class="copy-btn" onclick="copyCmd(this)">Copy</button>
            </div>
            <pre>cd /jetson-inference/python/training/detection/ssd
wget https://nvidia.box.com/shared/static/djf5w54rjvpqocsiztzaandq1m3avr7c.pth -O models/mobilenet-v1-ssd-mp-0_675.pth

# Download 100 images of target class (e.g. "Apple") to mounted USB drive
python3 open_images_downloader.py --max-images=100 --class-names "Apple" --data=/media/mobis/mobis_usb</pre>
          </div>
        </article>

        <article id="jetson-4-2-tl-train" class="module-card">
          <div class="module-header">
            <span class="module-badge">Module 4-2 • PyTorch SSD Fine-Tuning &amp; TensorRT ONNX Export</span>
            <span style="font-family:var(--font-mono); font-size:11.5px; color:var(--text-muted);">train_ssd.py • onnx_export.py • Live Inference</span>
          </div>
          <h3 class="module-title">4-2. Fine-Tune Custom SSD in PyTorch, Export ONNX &amp; Run Live Inference (4-2. 실습 학습)</h3>
          <p>
            Fine-tune the MobileNet-V1 SSD detector on your USB dataset with <code>train_ssd.py</code>, export the lowest-loss PyTorch <code>.pth</code> checkpoint to <code>ssd-mobilenet.onnx</code> via <code>onnx_export.py</code>, and run real-time TensorRT detection on the live CSI camera feed:
          </p>

          <div class="cmd-block">
            <div class="cmd-top">
              <span>Train Custom SSD in PyTorch, Export to ONNX &amp; Run Live CSI Inference</span>
              <button class="copy-btn" onclick="copyCmd(this)">Copy</button>
            </div>
            <pre>cd /jetson-inference/python/training/detection/ssd

# 1. Train for 5 epochs (or resume from a saved checkpoint with --resume=models/...)
python3 train_ssd.py --data=/media/mobis/mobis_usb --model-dir=models --batch-size=4 --epochs=5

# 2. Convert PyTorch (.pth) checkpoint to ONNX (ssd-mobilenet.onnx)
python3 onnx_export.py --model-dir=models

# 3. Test custom ONNX model on live CSI Camera (TensorRT auto-builds engine on first run)
detectnet.py --model=models/ssd-mobilenet.onnx --labels=models/labels.txt \
  --input-blob=input_0 --output-cvg=scores --output-bbox=boxes \
  --input-width=400 --input-height=300 csi://0</pre>
          </div>
        </article>

        <article id="jetson-5-dli" class="module-card">
          <div class="module-header">
            <span class="module-badge">Module 5 • NVIDIA DLI Certificates &amp; Docker Disk Recovery</span>
            <span style="font-family:var(--font-mono); font-size:11.5px; color:var(--text-muted);">DLI Free Courses • Reclaim 5GB SD Space</span>
          </div>
          <h3 class="module-title">5. Complete NVIDIA DLI Certificates &amp; Reclaim Docker Disk Space (5. Certification 관련 주의사항)</h3>
          <p>
            Enroll in NVIDIA Deep Learning Institute's two self-paced Jetson Nano certificate courses:
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><a href="https://courses.nvidia.com/courses/course-v1:DLI+S-RX-02+V2/about" target="_blank" rel="noopener" style="color:var(--accent); font-weight:600;">Getting Started with AI on Jetson Nano (DLI+S-RX-02+V2) ↗</a></li>
            <li><a href="https://courses.nvidia.com/courses/course-v1:DLI+C-IV-02+V1/" target="_blank" rel="noopener" style="color:var(--accent); font-weight:600;">Building Video AI Applications at the Edge on Jetson Nano (DeepStream) ↗</a></li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            <strong>Reclaim 5 GB from mismatched DLI Docker tags:</strong> The DLI lab instructions hardcode a JetPack 4.4 container tag. Running that pull command on a JetPack 4.5 microSD card downloads an unusable 5 GB image. List your local Docker images and delete the unused image ID to recover disk space:
          </p>

          <div class="cmd-block">
            <div class="cmd-top">
              <span>Inspect &amp; Remove Duplicate 5GB Docker Container Image</span>
              <button class="copy-btn" onclick="copyCmd(this)">Copy</button>
            </div>
            <pre># Check available SD card space and list downloaded Docker images
df -h
sudo docker images

# Remove the mismatched IMAGE ID to immediately recover ~5GB
sudo docker rmi -f &lt;IMAGE_ID&gt;
df -h</pre>
          </div>
        </article>

        <article id="jetson-6-tlt" class="module-card">
          <div class="module-header">
            <span class="module-badge">Module 6 • NVIDIA Transfer Learning Toolkit (TLT / TAO)</span>
            <span style="font-family:var(--font-mono); font-size:11.5px; color:var(--text-muted);">NGC Catalog • DetectNet_v2 • INT8 Calibration</span>
          </div>
          <h3 class="module-title">6. Scale Custom Detectors with NVIDIA TAO Toolkit &amp; INT8 Quantization (6. Transfer Learning 방법 &amp; 링크)</h3>
          <p>
            To reach higher production frame rates, train and prune custom detectors on a workstation or cloud GPU with the <strong>NVIDIA TAO Toolkit</strong> (formerly Transfer Learning Toolkit) and pretrained NGC backbones. Calibrate weights to FP16 or INT8 precision and deploy the compiled TensorRT engine onto the Jetson Nano.
          </p>
          <div class="resource-row">
            <a class="res-pill" href="https://developer.nvidia.com/transfer-learning-toolkit" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NVIDIA TLT / TAO Developer Portal ↗</a>
            <a class="res-pill" href="https://ngc.nvidia.com/catalog/models/nvidia:tlt_pretrained_object_detection" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NGC: TLT Pre-Trained Object Detection ↗</a>
            <a class="res-pill" href="https://ngc.nvidia.com/catalog/models/nvidia:tlt_pretrained_detectnet_v2" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NGC: TLT Pre-Trained DetectNet_v2 ↗</a>
          </div>
        </article>
      </section>
